---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../lib/schema';
import { ADS_LIVE } from '../lib/monetization';

/* --- Terms of use ---------------------------------------------------------
   Written to be read, and written to be true. The section that matters on a
   soundboard is "Sounds, Copyright and Who Owns What": most clips here are
   somebody else's intellectual property, hosted for reference and comment,
   and this page must not imply we can license them to anyone, because we
   cannot. Anything that reads like "all sounds are royalty free" would be
   both false and the exact claim that gets a site like this sued.

   TODO (owner): two deliberate omissions, both of which need a lawyer rather
   than a template.
     1. No governing-law / jurisdiction clause. Naming one is a real legal
        commitment about where disputes are heard, and guessing it from a
        postal address is not good enough.
     2. No arbitration or class-action clause, for the same reason.
   Nothing is stated on either, which is safer than stating the wrong thing.

   This page is plain-English policy, not legal advice, and it has not been
   reviewed by a lawyer. Worth doing before ad revenue makes the site a
   target.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
const EFFECTIVE = '10 September 2026';
const CONTACT_TO = 'dmca@bleepboard.com';

const schema = [
  breadcrumbs([
    ['Home', SITE.url + '/'],
    ['Terms of Use', SITE.url + '/terms/'],
  ]),
];
---
<Base
  title="Terms of Use | Bleepboard"
  description="The rules for using Bleepboard: what you may do with the sounds, who owns the clips, what we promise and what we do not. Written in plain English."
  schema={schema}
>
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a>
    <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
    <span class="here">Terms of Use</span>
  </nav>

  <section class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="tick"></span>Effective {EFFECTIVE}</p>
    <h1>Terms of Use</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The rules for using this site, written so you can actually read them. If something here
      is unclear, ask rather than guess: the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> goes to a
      person.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>The Short Version</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>Playing and downloading sounds is free, and no account is required.</li>
        <li>
          We do not own most of the clips on this site, so we cannot give you permission to use
          them. That permission is the rights holder's to give.
        </li>
        <li>
          Personal use is fine. Commercial use of a clip is between you and whoever owns it.
        </li>
        <li>
          If a clip is yours and you want it removed, the
          <a href="/dmca/">takedown page</a> explains how, and those requests are handled first.
        </li>
        <li>The site is provided as it is, with no promises that it will always work.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The rest of this page is the same thing at greater length. Using Bleepboard means you
        accept it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>What Bleepboard Is</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Bleepboard is a free soundboard. It hosts short audio clips, mostly from internet
        culture, each on its own page with a written explanation of where the clip came from.
        There is nothing to install and no account to create.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site is operated by Bleepboard, Office 36A, UFONE Tower, Blue Area, Islamabad,
        Pakistan.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Sounds, Copyright and Who Owns What</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        This is the part worth reading properly, because it is the part most soundboards are
        vague about.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The clips are not ours.</strong> Most sounds on this site originate in films,
        television, games, songs, streams and videos made by other people, and the rights in
        them stay with those people. Hosting a clip here does not transfer any rights to us,
        and it does not let us pass rights on to you. Anyone telling you a meme soundboard has
        made a clip royalty free is wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Why the clips are here at all.</strong> Each sound sits on a page that
        identifies it, explains its origin and comments on how it is used. The clips are short,
        they are hosted for reference and commentary, and they are not a substitute for the
        work they came from. Whether that is permitted use depends on where you are and what
        the clip is, and it is a judgement a court makes, not a claim we get to assert.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>"Free download" means free of charge.</strong> It does not mean free of
        copyright. The download button spares you a screen recorder. It does not hand you a
        licence.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What is ours.</strong> The written descriptions, the explainer articles, the
        site design, the code, the mascot and the artwork are Bleepboard's own work. Quote them
        with credit and a link. Do not scrape the corpus wholesale to fill somebody else's
        soundboard, which is the thing this site was built to be better than.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>If a clip is yours.</strong> Send a takedown notice and it comes down. The
        <a href="/dmca/">DMCA and takedown page</a> sets out exactly what a notice needs to
        contain. You do not need a lawyer to write one, and you will not get an argument from
        us about whether your own work is yours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>What You May Do With a Clip</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Playing a sound on this site, downloading it for yourself, and using it privately with
        friends, in a Discord call or over a video you are editing for fun, is what the site is
        for and nobody here has a problem with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Beyond that, the honest answer is that it depends on the clip and it is your call to
        make. Putting a copyrighted sound into a monetised video, an advert, a product or
        anything else commercial is between you and the rights holder. Platforms run their own
        automated matching too, so a clip that is fine socially can still get a video muted or
        claimed. We cannot clear any of that for you, and we will not pretend otherwise.
      </p>
      <p>Some things are not acceptable use of this site under any reading:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Using a clip to harass, threaten, impersonate or deceive somebody.</li>
        <li>
          Presenting our written descriptions or articles as your own work, or republishing
          them at scale.
        </li>
        <li>
          Automated scraping, bulk downloading, or anything else that treats the site as a free
          media pipe rather than a place people read.
        </li>
        <li>
          Attempting to break, overload or work around the site's technical limits, or using it
          in a way that breaks the law where you are.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Embedding Buttons</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every sound page carries an embed code, and you are welcome to use it. The embedded
        player keeps a visible link back to the sound's page on this site, and that link is the
        arrangement: keep it, and the embed is free to use. Stripping the attribution and
        passing the player off as your own is not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Embeds are provided as they are. If a clip is later removed after a takedown notice,
        the embed for it stops working, and that is a consequence of doing the right thing
        rather than a fault to report.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>What We Do Not Promise</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The site is provided as it is and as it happens to be available. We do not guarantee
        that it will be online, that every button will work, that a clip you used yesterday
        will still be here tomorrow, or that a page is free of mistakes.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do try hard on that last one. Descriptions are researched and corrected when they
        turn out to be wrong, and a wrong fact is treated as a bug rather than a cosmetic
        issue. Even so, this is a site about internet folklore, where the record is often thin
        and sometimes contested, so treat a page here as a well-sourced account rather than
        the final word.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sounds get removed sometimes, usually because a rights holder asked. There is no
        promise that any particular clip stays available, so if a sound matters to your work,
        keep your own copy.
      </p>
      <p>
        To the fullest extent the law allows, Bleepboard is not liable for loss or damage
        arising from your use of the site or of anything you downloaded from it, including how
        you chose to use a clip.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Links, Ads and Other People's Services</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Pages here link out to sources, wikis, videos and articles, because that is what makes
        an origin story checkable. Those sites are not ours and we are not responsible for what
        they do.
      </p>
      {ADS_LIVE ? (
        <p>
          This site carries advertising, which pays for hosting and for the time that goes into
          the write-ups. Ads are served by third parties who set their own cookies and
          identifiers, and the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> explains what that means
          and how to control it.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          There is currently no advertising on this site. If that changes, the
          <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> will say so before it happens, and this
          paragraph will change with it.
        </p>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Changes to These Terms</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        These terms will change as the site does. When they change materially, the effective
        date at the top of this page changes with them. There is no mailing list to notify,
        so the date is the honest signal, and continuing to use the site after a change means
        you accept the current version.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="contact">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Questions</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Anything about this page goes to <b class="mail">{CONTACT_TO}</b>, or through the
        <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>, which routes the common requests for you.
      </p>
      <p class="fine">
        This page describes how Bleepboard operates. It is not legal advice. If you need to
        know how a particular law applies to you or to a clip you want to use, ask a lawyer
        rather than a soundboard.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="btn-row back-row">
      <a class="btn" href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/dmca/">DMCA and Takedowns</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/contact/">Contact</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
